# Site-local settings for logbark, sourced via ". $0.cfg" on every run.
# Copy this file to "logbark.cfg" next to the script and adjust it --
# logbark.cfg itself is gitignored since it typically ends up holding
# device identifiers (MAC/IMSI/IMEI) you don't want in version control.

# IGNORES: "|"-joined regex alternatives; a line matching any of them is
# dropped instead of buffered. Matching is unanchored, so "some-chatty-daemon"
# drops every line mentioning it. Blank lines are always dropped regardless.
# The trailing "__END__|" is just a convenient sentinel to append to -- an
# empty alternative would match everything, so the script strips it.
    IGNORES=\
"some-chatty-daemon|"\
\
"__END__|"

# AP_ETHERS: "Label|Identifier|" pairs (padding on the label is cosmetic and
# gets trimmed) of known devices whose raw identifier is swapped for the
# friendly label wherever it appears in a buffered line. This is a lookup
# table only -- listing a device here annotates its lines, it does not drop
# them; use IGNORES for that. Terminate the list with "^$|__END__": the walk
# stops at "__END__", the "^$" is just the dummy label pairing with it.
    AP_ETHERS=\
"my-router      |AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF|"\
\
"^$|__END__"

# Optional: override the outgoing mail domain (default is "example.com",
# or whatever /etc/nullmailer/remotes resolves to if present).
#EMAIL_DOMAIN="example.com"

# Optional: command prefix used to run awk when not root (needed to
# read /etc/nullmailer/remotes). Leave unset to run unprivileged.
#PRIV_CMD="sudo"

# Optional: seconds to collect lines before flushing a batch.
#WAIT_TO_SEND=5

# Optional: where all runtime state is kept.
#RUNDIR=/tmp

# Optional: command prefix the FLUSH pass is launched through. Empty (the
# default) runs it as a plain background child of the invoking process, which
# is what you want almost everywhere.
#
# It exists for callers that run logbark inside a sandbox the flush cannot
# work in. The case in the wild is Debian 13 (trixie), whose rsyslog.service
# ships NoNewPrivileges=yes: every child inherits it, so the setuid bit on
# nullmailer's queue helper is stripped, the queue file is created root:root
# 0600, and nullmailer-send -- running as user "mail" -- can never read it.
# Mail is accepted and queued, then silently never delivered. Sending it from
# a transient unit instead re-parents the flush to PID 1, outside that sandbox:
#
#FLUSH_WRAPPER="systemd-run --quiet --collect --property=ExitType=cgroup --unit=logbark-flush-$$"
#
# Three things that recipe depends on:
#
#   - RUNDIR must NOT be under /tmp if the calling unit has PrivateTmp=yes.
#     The caller would append to its own private /tmp while the transient unit
#     reads the real one, and the flush would find an empty buffer and mail
#     nothing. Pin RUNDIR to a real path (e.g. /var/lib/logbark) alongside
#     this setting.
#   - The script must be invoked by an absolute path, or be reachable from the
#     caller's $PWD -- systemd-run rejects a relative executable. Anything
#     started from rsyslog already satisfies this.
#   - ExitType=cgroup is not optional with an MTA that delivers from a child
#     of the submitting process, which is what exim does by default. Without
#     it the transient unit is finished the instant sendmail exits, and
#     KillMode defaults to control-group, so systemd kills exim's forked
#     delivery process before it gets anywhere. exim logs the "<=" that
#     accepts the message and no "=>" at all, and nothing is delivered until
#     the next queue run -- minutes later, or a full QUEUEINTERVAL. Setting
#     it keeps the unit alive until every process in its cgroup has exited.
#     It costs nothing for nullmailer or postfix, which only spool and
#     return, leaving delivery to a daemon that was never in the unit.
#
# Use "$$" (expanded here, when the cfg is sourced) rather than a fixed unit
# name: the debounce lock is released before the flush runs, so two flushes can
# legitimately overlap and a fixed name would collide.
